 FDA didn't  really envision that there you think  yeah they're using ingredients and  techniques that often aren't even  contemplated and they're sort of staying  one step ahead of the regulations but  these moments of conflict or crisis you  know tonka beans a few years ago became  you know a lot of chefs too had no idea  that tonka beans have been on the FDA  not approved list since the 1950s  because of an ingredient in it as an  anticoagulant and there was a concern  that it could cause hemorrhaging tonka  bean is an ingredient sort of tastes  like vanilla and it's used widely in  pastries and ice creams in Europe and  chefs here like grant a cat's very well  known chef three Michelin stars and  first restaurant Alinea in Chicago  we're using it and had no idea and then  there's spice cabinets we're being  raided by the FDA and it was that was  that was now several years ago but there  are things like this occur frequently  and raw milk is another one another  flashpoint you actually talked about raw  milk and a store here in Venice called  Rossum and their whole motto was we're  not gonna sell it to you unless it's  dangerous but what's interesting is that  there's a complete inversion of the idea  of what's safe and what's dangerous and  in that community which is a very  extreme community where I wanted to look  at it the idea was you know anything  that the government has come within a  mile of is dangerous that means that  it's been sprayed with you know  antibacterials you know the that it has  been chemically treated in ways that  that community is not comfortable with  and feels is unsafe and unhealthy for  them and so the government idea of safe  food and and often the foodie idea of  safe food is completely inverted you  know it seemed like you were a little  bit skeptical which may be a good thing  because you know I don't want to eat  something that could potentially kill me  I personally don't take it to the  extreme of you know demanding that I be